{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "abacusai/dracarys-llama-3.1-70b-instruct", "content": "Oregon's pioneering experiment with decriminalizing drugs is set to expire, and a new law will take its place, making possession of small amounts of hard drugs a crime once again. The new law, HB4002, will give individuals caught with illicit drugs the option to either face charges or undergo treatment, which includes completing a behavioral health program and participating in a \"deflection program\" to avoid fines.\n\nUnder the new law, personal-use possession will be considered a misdemeanor, punishable by up to six months in jail. The law aims to make it easier for police to crack down on public drug use and introduces harsher penalties for selling drugs near public spaces such as parks.\n\nThe law encourages, but does not require, counties to create treatment alternatives to divert individuals from the criminal justice system and towards addiction and mental health services. Proponents of the law argue that this allows counties to develop programs based on their resources, while opponents argue that it may create a confusing and inequitable patchwork of policy.\n\nPortland police chief Bob Day explained that the criteria for deflection are narrow, and if a person meets the requirements, officers will connect them with a mobile behavioral health outreach team. However, if the team cannot make contact within 30 minutes, the individual will be taken to jail.\n\n***\nP.P.S. As the new law takes effect, Oregon residents of all ages will be affected, from young adults to the elderly. The country's approach to drug policy will be closely watched, and it remains to be seen how this will impact the lives of those struggling with addiction. In the meantime, people will still go about their daily routines, shopping for groceries and eating meals, but the new law will be a constant presence, much like the taste of a bitter tongue.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.02678580768406391}, {"token": "'s", "logprob": -0.11135436594486237}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.46469035744667053}, {"token": " experiment", "logprob": -0.525070309638977}, {"token": " with", "logprob": -0.4066064655780792}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.0006679738871753216}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -1.1920930376163597e-07}, {"token": "izing", "logprob": -0.00011230145901208743}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -0.06664849072694778}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.19454900920391083}, {"token": " set", "logprob": -0.18337492644786835}, {"token": " to", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " expire", "logprob": -1.5836694240570068}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.6592147946357727}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -0.7734373807907104}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0003333647327963263}, {"token": " new", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " law", "logprob": -7.152560215217818e-07}, {"token": " will", "logprob": -0.18865321576595306}, {"token": " take", "logprob": -0.45417600870132446}, {"token": " its", "logprob": -0.25193947553634644}, {"token": " place", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.08212297409772873}, {"token": " making", "logprob": -0.12557001411914825}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -1.3753187656402588}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920930376163597e-07}, {"token": " small", "logprob": -0.0018120126333087683}, {"token": " amounts", "logprob": -4.541977250482887e-05}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " hard", "logprob": -0.0069221872836351395}, {"token": " drugs", "logprob": -1.919287933560554e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0008682800689712167}, {"token": " crime", "logprob": -0.00019748968770727515}, {"token": " once", "logprob": -0.0023105591535568237}, {"token": " again", "logprob": -0.006715324241667986}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.01633381098508835}, {"token": " The", "logprob": -0.3905137777328491}, {